BLAKE3, carried as a subtree.

    https://github.com/BLAKE3-team/BLAKE3
    77b257eee7da5cd608eaf6be8343d3a4c9776af2, 2026-08-05

What is here is the contents of the upstream `c/` directory, unmodified, minus
two things: the hand-written assembly (blake3_*_x86-64_*.S and .asm) and the
upstream CMake files.

The assembly is left out on purpose. Upstream offers two ways to get the same
SIMD kernels -- assembly, or C with intrinsics -- and they compute the same
function. The C ones build with one toolchain rule per file, the same rule this
tree already uses for the SHA-256 kernels next door, and they cross-compile
without a second assembler in the mix. What that costs is a few percent on
large inputs. A block header is 80 bytes and a transaction is rarely more than
a few hundred, so the single-block path is what this chain actually runs, and
the assembly does not touch it.

AVX-512 is compiled out (BLAKE3_NO_AVX512). The tree probes for SSE4.1 and AVX2
and has no probe for AVX-512, and adding a code path nothing here can test is
worse than not having it. blake3_avx512.c is carried anyway so that turning it
on later is a build-system change and not another import.

Consensus note: every node must agree on the hash of a block, so the answer
this library gives cannot depend on which kernel the dispatcher picked. It does
not -- the SIMD paths are the same function, and blake3_dispatch.c chooses
between them by CPUID at runtime. test/test_blake3 checks the official vectors,
and checks them again with the dispatcher forced down to the portable path, so
that claim is tested rather than assumed.
